At that time the Hunt brothers of Texas spent nearly a
billion dollars trying to corner the market in silver.  They
amassed a vast hoard of silver but they also discovered
that over the years vast amounts of silver was made into
sterling silverware, jewelry and such.  Push the price up
enough and people sell their old silver for melt value.
For what it's worth, the price of silver ever since has been
maintained by folks selling old stuff for melt.  At some
point it may actually be possible to corner the market
with a few bollion dollars if the stored silverware and such
goes down enough in quantity.  Don't expect that to
happen in the next couple of decades, though.

Interesting tidbit about silver and gold - All of the gold
ever put into Fort Knox in the US is still there.  The
similar hoard of US silver was at West Point.  That
hoard was sold across the 1990s and is now gone. One
huge hoard of silver in national treasuries no longer
exists and I wouldn't be surprised in the same were true
in lots of nations.  Golkd, though, lots of nations still
have tons of it.

Anyways, the price of gold tends to track the price of
silver and vice versa.  If something happens to drive the
price of either it ends up driving the price of both.  The
price of gold spiked because someone was manipulating
the price of silver back then.

